{"author":"Rebar Bot","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"fields":{"description":"## Status: investigation complete, this ticket is now remediation\n\nThe investigation this ticket demanded has been performed and is recorded in the comments. Two of its premises were **refuted**, and the correction changes what the fix must be.\n\n## Established findings\n\n**The count was wrong.** Not ~52 failures: **51 total**, of which only **4** are POSIX semaphore (`SemLock`) denials. The other 47 are unrelated environmental failures with five distinct causes of their own, split to `985f-0ec5-a61c-46ee`. The original \"~52 SemLock errors\" reading came from a shared signature in the traceback tail, not from 52 distinct semaphore denials.\n\n**The runner-level population is exactly 0.** This ticket asked for the failure count to be attributed between runner-level parallelism and test-level `multiprocessing` use. The answer is unambiguous: pytest-xdist brings its worker pool up **successfully** under the sandbox and reaches 99% of the suite. All 4 SemLock failures are test-level. Consequently **worker count is not a remedy for any part of this**, and candidate 1 in the original framing is eliminated. Note also that `PYTEST_WORKERS=1` does not bypass xdist at all — `make test` hardcodes `-n $(PYTEST_WORKERS)`, so that lever never tested what it appeared to test.\n\n**Mechanism, probe-confirmed.** The sandbox denies Darwin `sem_open` with `EPERM` (errno 1). CPython has no fallback path because `sem_init` returns `ENOSYS` (errno 78) on Darwin. This is not a backing-file or filesystem denial, so no path redirection helps. **No start method escapes it** — and notably `multiprocessing.dummy.Pool`, despite being thread-backed, fails identically, because it still constructs a `SemLock`.\n\n## What that means for the fix\n\nThese 4 tests exercise `multiprocessing` behaviour as the thing under test. The capability is therefore part of the contract, not an execution detail — which is exactly the case the original framing identified as the one where silently skipping **would** weaken the gate. Combined with the finding that no start method and no threading substitute avoids the denial, there is no configuration that makes these 4 pass under the sandbox.\n\nThe correct outcome is therefore: **probe for the capability, and where it is absent, skip these 4 loudly and countably** while they continue to run normally in CI. That restores `make verify` to producing a real verdict locally without reducing anything CI executes.\n\n## Dependency on `985f` — same file, deliberate ordering\n\n`985f-0ec5-a61c-46ee` remediates the other 47 failures and centralizes its work in **`tests/conftest.py`**, building the capability-probe and counted-skip accounting machinery there. This ticket needs exactly that machinery and touches the same file.\n\n**These two must not be worked in parallel.** `985f` lands first and establishes the probe and accounting framework; this ticket then adds a semaphore probe to it. Doing the reverse, or doing both at once, duplicates the machinery and conflicts in `tests/conftest.py`.\n\n## Approach\n\n1. Add a `sem_open` capability probe alongside the probes established by `985f` — attempt the operation and observe the result, never inferring from environment names.\n2. Gate the 4 affected tests on that probe, so they run wherever the capability exists and skip where it does not.\n3. Route those skips through the counted, reported signal `985f` establishes, so the local run states plainly that 4 tests could not execute and why.\n4. Document the local-versus-CI difference in `docs/local-dev-env.md`, including the explicit statement that worker count is unrelated — so the next person under this failure does not repeat the eliminated hypothesis.\n\n## Testing strategy\n\nTDD: the failing test comes first.\n\n- A test asserts the probe reports absence when semaphore creation is denied, simulating the denial directly rather than depending on the ambient environment. This must hold on a machine where the capability **is** available, or the test only passes by accident of where it runs.\n- A test asserts the probe reports availability on a host that has the capability, so the probe cannot trivially pass by always returning \"absent\" — which would silently disable the 4 tests in CI, the one outcome that must not happen.\n- A test asserts the skip is counted and surfaced in the run summary.\n- Regression evidence: under the sandbox, the SemLock failure population drops from 4 to 0, with 4 accounted skips; in an environment with the capability, all 4 execute and pass.\n\n## Acceptance criteria\n\n1. `make verify` runs to completion and produces a pass/fail verdict on a sandboxed local workstation.\n2. CI continues to select and execute exactly what it selects today. None of the 4 tests is skipped in CI — asserted by the probe-reports-available test, not by inspection.\n3. The 4 locally unrunnable tests are reported explicitly and countably in the local run's summary, never silently skipped.\n4. Capability detection is a genuine runtime probe. No hostname, username, or CI-variable heuristic appears in the remedy. This also keeps the fix portable: the probe behaves correctly under any CI provider and under none.\n5. `docs/local-dev-env.md` documents the local behaviour, how it differs from CI, and records that worker count is not a factor.\n6. No unadmitted mechanism is added. Any genuinely necessary addition carries a `# mechanism-ok:` marker at its definition site naming this ticket; the baseline is not hand-edited.\n\n## Out of scope\n\nThe other 47 failures (`985f-0ec5-a61c-46ee`). Changing `make test`'s xdist invocation — the runner is not implicated, and modifying it would be a fix aimed at a hypothesis the evidence eliminated."}},"env_id":"eea68b7f-6697-488b-ac52-744e2ed8e96f","event_type":"EDIT","timestamp":1789691053473125301,"uuid":"9a1a3dbe-e41a-4124-a102-0014ebf61e41"}